/*
DUCK EXAMPLES: READING GUIDE

Duck is in the design stage. These are specification examples, not
compiler-validated programs. Each numbered .dk lesson is independent:
do not compile the whole Examples directory as one program. Many lessons
declare their own Main and reuse container names.

Read in this order:
  Tutorial/01..06       Values, functions, containers, and access.
  Collections/01..05    Arrays, bounds, maps, ranges, and loop chaining.
  Ownership/01..09      Owners, aliases, transfers, factories, destruction.
  Concurrency/01..04    Resource synchronization and explicit $ protection.
  Containers/01..05     Inheritance, duck calls, properties, initialization.
  Features/01..05       Enums, reflection, lambdas, baking, built-in tests.
  Lifecycle/01..03      Custom scopes and frame lifetimes.
  Markup/01..02         Components and repeated children.
  CompileTime/01..02    Definitions and macros.
  Games/01             Combining ownership, collections, and safe calls.

FailureCases contains deliberately broken .dk examples. Read each header
before using one. Some are invalid code, some fail at runtime, and others
demonstrate logic bugs or scheduling-dependent results. Expected diagnostic
wording is descriptive, not a promise of exact compiler output.

DesignQuestions.dk records cases the current design does not settle.
Do not treat these as established rules or runnable tests.

Existing Basic and Arrays examples predate this tutorial and are preserved.
The project README is the language design reference.

Conventions:
  EXPECT: the intended observable result under the documented design.
  PITFALL: a tempting mistake and why it matters.
  FIX: the safe or correct alternative.
  Output comments describe values, not exact formatting of Print.
  Invalid alternatives in successful lessons remain commented out.

Key rules:
  : infers a local type; :: allocates on the heap or transfers ownership.
  Transferring ownership makes the previous owning variable null.
  Existing aliases follow the resource under its new owner.
  Only the current owner can transfer ownership.
  Destruction nulls aliases everywhere; collection entries remain.
  Cleanup waits for existing protected uses to finish.
  Returning heap ownership requires return ::resource.
  Resource reads wait for preceding work affecting that resource.
  $ orders work and locks the resources the block uses, including callees.
  A check and a modification need $ for an atomic multi-step guarantee.
*/
